296 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 82 0 R
/E (      The Seediq people were once massacred in the Wushe Incident. The Wushe Incident was one of the most important events in Taiwan's aboriginal resistance to rule during the Japanese rule and a traumatic event in Taiwan's history. This incident has raised concern for the rights and protection of the indigenous peoples and has led to a greater understanding of the Seediq culture.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
297 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 627 0 R
/E (Causes - Unfair treatment of indigenous peoples by the government )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
298 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 82 0 R
/E (      The government enforced a policy of "cultural assimilation", forcing the unification of the way of life, language, and beliefs of the indigenous peoples. The implementation of this policy had a great impact on the cultural heritage and livelihood of the indigenous people. In addition, the exclusive occupation of the land and resources of the aborigines led to the intensification of the conflict between the aborigines and the government.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
299 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 628 0 R
/E (The trigger - the "Toast" on October 7, 1930)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
300 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 82 0 R
/E (      According to the police records, the Wushe Incident began with a wedding banquet at the Mahepo community, where a local police officer, Katsumi Yoshimura, injured Mona Rudo's eldest son, Dado Mona, with his baton in a dispute over a toast, leading to a fight between the two. After the incident, Yoshimura chose to refuse to accept Mona Ludo and threatened to report him to his superiors. This provoked fears among the clan and dissatisfaction with the colonial government, which led to a decision to revolt.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
301 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 629 0 R
/E (The process - May 27, 1930)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
302 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 82 0 R
/E (      During the regular field day at the Wushe public school, Mahepo chief Mona Rudo led more than 300 people to fight. The attack was led by Mona Rudo, the leader of Mahepo, who led more than 300 Seediq warriors, resulting in the death of many Japanese. The incident angered the government and sparked strong military retaliation, with fierce clashes between the two sides, including the use of chemical gas, which was internationally banned. The incident angered the colonial government and triggered strong military retaliation. Both sides engaged in fierce clashes, and the government forces even used internationally banned chemical gas in their counterattack, resulting in serious casualties and tragic losses on both sides.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
